Q: How can I validate hardware fitment tolerances in Python? A: <p>The problem with your current approach is that you're mixing measurement logic with tolerance rules. Once you separate them, adding manufacturer support becomes trivial.</p>
<p>I split this into three parts: a <code>ToleranceProfile</code> dataclass to hold the rules, a <code>PROFILES</code> dict for manufacturer specific values, and a checker function that returns a rich result instead of just a string.</p>
<pre><code>from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from typing import Optional

@dataclass
class ToleranceProfile:
    name: str
    good_fit_max: float        # max clearance for a snug fit
    loose_fit_max: float       # max clearance before it rattles
    spacer_compensates: float  # how much a washer can recover
    notes: Optional[str] = field(default=None)


PROFILES = {
    &quot;generic&quot;: ToleranceProfile(
        name=&quot;Generic&quot;,
        good_fit_max=0.5,
        loose_fit_max=1.5,
        spacer_compensates=0.8,
    ),
    &quot;abloy&quot;: ToleranceProfile(
        name=&quot;Abloy&quot;,
        good_fit_max=0.3,
        loose_fit_max=1.0,
        spacer_compensates=0.5,
        notes=&quot;Abloy cylinders require precise fitment&quot;
    ),
    &quot;yale&quot;: ToleranceProfile(
        name=&quot;Yale&quot;,
        good_fit_max=0.6,
        loose_fit_max=1.8,
        spacer_compensates=1.0,
    ),
}


def fitment_status(spindle_hole: float, cylinder_diameter: float,
                   manufacturer: str = &quot;generic&quot;) -&gt; dict:

    profile = PROFILES.get(manufacturer.lower())
    if not profile:
        raise ValueError(f&quot;Unknown manufacturer: '{manufacturer}'. &quot;
                         f&quot;Available: {list(PROFILES.keys())}&quot;)

    clearance = spindle_hole - cylinder_diameter

    if clearance &lt; 0:
        status = &quot;Interference&quot;
        recommendation = &quot;Cylinder is too large for this escutcheon&quot;

    elif clearance &lt;= profile.good_fit_max:
        status = &quot;Good Fit&quot;
        recommendation = &quot;No action needed&quot;

    elif clearance &lt;= profile.loose_fit_max:
        status = &quot;Loose Fit&quot;
        if clearance &lt;= profile.spacer_compensates:
            recommendation = &quot;Spacer washer will fix this&quot;
        else:
            recommendation = &quot;Too loose even with a spacer&quot;

    else:
        status = &quot;Poor Fit&quot;
        recommendation = &quot;Replace escutcheon or use a different cylinder&quot;

    return {
        &quot;status&quot;: status,
        &quot;clearance_mm&quot;: round(clearance, 3),
        &quot;manufacturer_profile&quot;: profile.name,
        &quot;recommendation&quot;: recommendation,
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Your original call still works with no changes:</p>
<pre><code>print(fitment_status(11.0, 10.7))
# {'status': 'Good Fit', 'clearance_mm': 0.3, 
#  'manufacturer_profile': 'Generic', 
#  'recommendation': 'No action needed'}
</code></pre>
<p>And now you can pass a manufacturer:</p>
<pre><code>print(fitment_status(11.0, 10.7, manufacturer=&quot;abloy&quot;))
# {'status': 'Loose Fit', 'clearance_mm': 0.3,
#  'manufacturer_profile': 'Abloy',
#  'recommendation': 'Spacer washer will fix this'}
</code></pre>
<p>When you scale to 20+ manufacturers, move the profiles out to a JSON file and load them with:</p>
<pre><code>import json

def load_profiles(path: str) -&gt; dict:
    with open(path) as f:
        data = json.load(f)
    return {k: ToleranceProfile(**v) for k, v in data.items()}
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>dataclass</code> keeps it structured without over-engineering it - adding a new manufacturer is just adding a few lines to <code>PROFILES</code> or the JSON file, no logic changes needed.</p>
<p>Let me know if it's works for you?</p>
